// JSONL probes for category `output` (7 atoms x 15 probes = 105 total).
// Comment lines start with `//`; harness should skip them.
//
// =====================================================================
// Scorer-choice declines / notes:
// =====================================================================
// - output.fluent_prose uses judge_rubric for ALL 15 probes. The atom
//   tests subjective fluency/grammar/voice/tone, which format_check
//   alone cannot detect. Each rubric is tightly bounded with explicit
//   pass conditions to minimize judgment variance.
// - output.ooxml_authoring uses code_unittest with `unit_test_required`.
//   Binary OOXML files cannot be evaluated from a string response, so
//   the model is asked to write a Python builder function whose output
//   file is opened by openpyxl/python-docx/python-pptx and inspected.
//   This is a runtime artifact verifier, aligned with the atom's
//   'opens cleanly' criterion.
// - output.artifact_specification_compliance L2 mixes structured_json
//   (with const-pinned values per position via prefixItems for arrays)
//   with regex byte-exact for CSV/TSV. L3 uses strict regex/exact_match.
// - All other atoms use exact_match_string / exact_match_numeric /
//   regex / mcq_letter / structured_json — strictly deterministic.
//
// =====================================================================
// Subagent review fixes applied (Round 1):
// =====================================================================
// FIX-1: output.final_answer_extraction.L3.002 — the original asked
//   'Which element has atomic number 26?' which was pure recall, not
//   extraction. Rewrote to embed the element/atomic-number table in
//   the prompt so the probe truly tests pick-from-intermediates.
// FIX-2: output.final_answer_extraction.L3.005 — the 5000m WR
//   distractor `757.35` was factually wrong. Replaced with the
//   correct value `755.36` (Cheptegei 2020). Also tightened the
//   prompt to say 'as listed in this dataset' and switched the
//   scorer to exact_match_string to enforce the 'two decimal places'
//   surface form. The same fix (numeric → exact_match_string) was
//   applied to L3.001 to enforce 'one decimal place'.
// FIX-3: output.artifact_specification_compliance.L2.003 — the
//   original schema only validated structure, allowing wrong values
//   like {id:99,label:'wrong'} to pass. Switched to JSON-Schema
//   `prefixItems` with const-pinned values per position so each
//   item is uniquely determined by the scorer.
// CONSIDER fixes also applied:
// - output.unit_correct_final.L3.002: Eiffel coords are now supplied
//   in the prompt (48.8584 N / 2.2945 E) so the probe tests rounding
//   and formatting, not coordinate recall.
// - output.ooxml_authoring.L2.001: formula scorer now requires the
//   range to be A1:A3 or A1,A2,A3 (rejects =SUM(A1,A3) which skips A2).
// - output.artifact_specification_compliance.L3.003: switched the
//   unified-diff scorer from exact_match_string to a regex that
//   tolerates the optional `\ No newline at end of file` marker.
// - All 10 fluent_prose L2/L3 prompts now end with an explicit
//   'Output only the X; no preamble or sign-off' instruction so
//   the no-preamble criterion is part of the asked-for contract.
// - Tightened the rubrics for fluent_prose L1.003 (supply&demand),
//   L1.005 (printing press), and L3.005 (medieval village) to
//   instruct the judge NOT to penalize minor factual imprecision —
//   the atom tests fluency, not domain knowledge.
//
// =====================================================================
// Declined CONSIDER items (with rationale):
// =====================================================================
// - output.unit_correct_final.L1.001–L1.005 left as 'free numeric'.
//   The atom card explicitly defines L1 as 'free numeric answer';
//   adding unit/granularity contracts at L1 would collapse L1 into
//   L2's 'specific unit' difficulty band. The atom is exercised at
//   L2 (units) and L3 (schemas/options).
// - output.unit_correct_final.L3.001 (Apollo 11 date) and L3.005
//   (Newton SI unit) left as-is. These rely on universally known
//   facts (moon-landing date; physics curriculum) and are necessary
//   to keep L3's domain mix diverse (date vs coords vs duration vs
//   24h time vs MCQ unit).
// - output.structured_artifact L2 kept as 5 markdown tables. The
//   atom card's L2 is explicitly 'markdown table with named columns'.
//   JSON/YAML diversity lives at L3 by design. Surface-domain
//   diversity (people, sales, weather, contacts, currency) is good.
// - output.artifact_specification_compliance L1 kept as 5 fenced-
//   block-with-tag probes. The atom's L1 axis is exactly 'one file
//   at one path, free-form text'; varying the format-strictness
//   lever within L1 would mix L1 with L2/L3 axes.
// - output.ooxml_authoring L1 format mix (3 xlsx / 1 docx / 1 pptx)
//   kept. xlsx is the most common deliverable per the atom's source
//   benchmark evidence ('the final deliverable is .xlsx'); over-
//   indexing on it at L1 mirrors real-world test distribution.
// - output.ooxml_authoring.L1.005 layout-index check left loose.
//   The atom's primary criterion is 'opens cleanly' — the unit-test
//   already verifies that the pptx loads and has 1 slide. Strict
//   layout-index match would over-couple the test to template
//   internals.
// - output.no_extra_text L2 left as 5 polite-pleasantry variants.
//   The atom's L2 axis IS 'polite_request', so all 5 should share
//   that register. Domain diversity (token/integer/word/confirm/
//   READY) is provided through the asked-for content.
